[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Berlin Defense, Rio Gambit Accepted"]
[Start "[0,0,0]"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. Re1 {White can avoid the immediate d4 Berlin endgame and attack the e4 knight first. Seirawan calls this whole Berlin family attractive because even these deviations give Black clear, forcing replies rather than a passive Spanish squeeze.} Nd6 6. Bxc6 (6. Nxe5 Nxe5 7. Rxe5+ Be7 {This is another weak-file branch. The pawn structure is very symmetrical, so Black should value easy development, not hunt for ghosts.}) dxc6 7. Nxe5 Be7 8. d4 O-O {Your deck stops here. Black has restored material balance, developed safely, and can continue with ...Re8, ...Bf6, or ...Nf5 depending on White's setup.} 9. Nc3 Re8 10. Bf4 Be6 {Keep improving pieces. In these Rio structures, the usual Berlin temptation is to over-defend; instead, complete development and make White prove the initiative.} *

[Event "Weak Opening GM Lesson - Berlin Anti-Systems"]
[Site "YouTube"]
