[Annotator "GM Yasser Seirawan; transcript-derived notes"]
[Source "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Mw8U3Lyy6Vg"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Berlin Defense"]
[Start "[0,0,0]"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. Bxc6 (4. d3 Bc5 {Seirawan notes that if White answers the e4 threat with d3, the usual c3/d4 Spanish plan has been delayed. Black should be happy to develop actively.} 5. Bxc6 dxc6) (4. d4 exd4 5. O-O Be7 {Against the Nyholm-style d4 sideline in your file, the reference move is simple development. Do not grab on e4 if it helps White's initiative.}) (4. Qe2 Bc5 {Against Qe2, put the bishop on the active diagonal. Your weak deck specifically flags ...Bc5 as the reference move.}) dxc6 {Early Bxc6 is not a refutation of the Berlin. Recapture toward the center, keep e5 stable, and remember that the bishop pair plus central structure give Black real play.} 5. d3 Bd6 {The deck's reference move. The bishop supports e5, eyes h2, and prepares normal development.} 6. Nbd2 O-O 7. Nc4 Re8 8. O-O c5 {A representative plan: once the e5 point is secure, Black can claim space and stop White from getting a free d4/e4 bind.} *
